.TH GP-WSWEEP 1 "2026-07-09" "gitplus" "gitplus Manual"
.SH NAME
gp-wsweep \- remove worktrees (and their branches) merged into the base, or detached
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gp wsweep
[\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-force\fR]
[\fB\-b\fR|\fB\-\-base\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The worktree analogue of
.BR gp-sweep (1).
Removes merged
.I worktrees
\(em the linked working directories \(em
.B and
deletes the underlying branch, same as running
.BR gp-sweep (1)
right afterward \(em you are almost always done with both at once. A
detached-HEAD worktree has no branch, so only the worktree goes.
.PP
Like gp-sweep, it fetches first and judges "merged" against the base's remote
tip
.RI ( origin/<base> ),
so it works without pulling the base locally and from any worktree.
.PP
Safe by default: a
.I branch-backed
worktree (and its branch) is removed only when its HEAD is already an
ancestor of the base's remote tip (verified with
.BR "git merge-base \-\-is-ancestor" ),
so no unmerged work is lost. Squash- and rebase-merged branches are caught
too, directly or through a stack: when the ancestor test says no, every
merged PR's head/base pair is fetched once and closed over locally to see
whether the branch's PR reaches the base through a chain of merges (needs
.BR gh ;
offline it falls back to the ancestor test alone).
.PP
A
.I detached-HEAD
worktree skips both of those checks and is removed unconditionally in safe
mode too \(em it holds no branch, so there is no unmerged-branch risk to
protect against, just an abandoned checkout location (commonly left behind by
.BR gp-switch (1)
freeing a branch from it). Still protected against surprise data loss:
.B "git worktree remove"
(without
.BR \-\-force )
refuses on its own if the worktree is dirty, so uncommitted changes there are
never silently dropped \(em only a clean, abandoned detached checkout goes.
.PP
The branch delete uses
.B \-D
rather than
.B \-d
on purpose, same reasoning as
.BR gp-sweep (1):
.B \-d
rechecks mergedness against your
.I current
branch, which wrongly refuses a branch merged upstream when your local base is
stale; the checks above are done instead, and trusted. The main worktree
(always listed first) and the worktree you are currently in are never touched
or deleted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Remove every worktree except the main one and the current one, regardless of
merge status, with
.BR "git worktree remove \-\-force" .
This also drops worktrees that have uncommitted changes, discarding those
changes.
.B Branches are not deleted in force mode
\(em that would compound two separately destructive actions into one flag; run
.B gp sweep \-f
afterward if you want those gone too.
.B "It discards uncommitted work"
\(em preview with
.B \-n
first.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-base " " \fINAME\fR
Base branch to compare against instead of origin's default.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
List what would be removed without changing anything.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B gp wsweep
Remove worktrees (and their branches) whose HEAD is merged into the base (safe).
.TP
.B gp wsweep \-f \-n
Preview a force sweep of every worktree except main and current.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gp-sweep (1),
.BR gp-sync (1),
.BR gp-pr (1),
.BR gp-done (1),
.BR git-worktree (1)
